# Introduction to Python Installation

## Introduction

Python is a widely used, high-level programming language known for its readability, versatility, and large ecosystem of libraries. It is used across web development, data science, automation, artificial intelligence, and scientific computing. This documentation walks a new user through installing Python on a computer, verifying that the installation was successful, and resolving common problems that come up along the way.

This guide is written for beginners who are installing Python for the first time, but it can also serve as a quick reference for anyone setting up a new machine.

## Software Requirements

Before installing Python, confirm that your system meets the following requirements:

| Requirement | Details |
|---|---|
| Operating System | Windows 10/11, macOS 11+, or a modern Linux distribution (Ubuntu 20.04+, Fedora, etc.) |
| Disk Space | At least 100 MB free (more if installing additional packages) |
| RAM | 2 GB minimum (4 GB or more recommended) |
| Internet Connection | Required to download the installer and packages |
| Administrator/Root Access | Recommended, so environment variables (PATH) can be updated during install |

### Choosing a Python Version

- Use **Python 3.x** — Python 2 reached end of life in January 2020 and should not be used for new projects.
- Download the latest stable release from the official source: https://www.python.org/downloads/

Continue to `Installation.md` for step-by-step installation instructions.